\chapter*{Introduction}
\label{chap:introduction}

Roughly ten years ago, I, the author of this thesis, had just started my 
undergraduate studies of physics at the Karlsruhe Institute of Technology. I 
still remember well how i was trying to keep up with all the different lectures 
I attended. Sometimes, when the work load would be overwhelming, I would go for 
a walk at night. I would find a bench to sit down on and look up into the night 
sky.

My undergraduate years seem impossibly far back to me now. I invite you to think
back to what your life was like ten years ago. So many things are not what they 
used to be. Some things have changed for the better, and some have changed for 
the worse. Moving past the ten year mark and personal anecdotes, the world has 
shifted dramatically in the recent century. The story of the human technological
advance is nothing short of awe-inspiring. A person born in the late 19th 
century grew up before the invention of airplanes. Just 60 years later, Neil 
Armstrong set a foot on the moon. 60 years later still, a commercial smartphone 
can perform more complex and faster calculations than the Apollo 11 navigation 
computers.

What would Magellan or Columbus say to GPS sattelites? Would the wanderings of 
Odysseus have been a similar epos had he had access to a sextant? Our 
ever-evolving culture and knowledge of the world makes it impossible to answer 
this today. Still, despite all the contrast throughout time and space, there 
exists one thing that unites you with Magellan, with Homer, and with me.

% Delete up to here?

Since the beginning of documented history we have looked to the stars. Whether
it be for spiritual guidance or earthly navigation, the twinkling image of 
faraway worlds has inspired a sense of wonder in us. We are curious about the 
universe and want to know how things function. This curiosity has led us to 
develop tools to test and describe the nature around us. It is the driving force
behind our technological prowess, and what makes us human.

Knowing this, one might say that the study of the cosmos is a frustrating 
endeavour. We cannot "test" the universe for anything. Given the sheer vastness 
of space we are forced to take on the role of a passive observer, relying on 
observation rather than experiment.  

